@@ -0,0 +1,134 @@
+# Deploying the USDC Tip Jar to IPFS
+
+The site ships as a fully static export (`packages/nextjs/out/`) that targets **Base (8453)**.
+Nothing in this repo uploads automatically. Every step below is run by hand, and every step has a check.
+
+How the build is configured:
+
+| Setting | Where | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true` | `next.config.ts` | `output: "export"`, `trailingSlash: true` (each route becomes `route/index.html`), `images.unoptimized` |
+| `NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true` | `scaffold.config.ts` | target network changes from the local fork (31337) to Base (8453). The burner wallet is hidden automatically. |
+| `NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL` | `utils/scaffold-eth/getMetadata.ts` | origin used in `og:url` / `og:image` (without it, they would point at `localhost`) |
+| Build guard | `next.config.ts` | the IPFS build **fails** if TipJar has no Base entry in `deployedContracts.ts` or `NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL` isn't `https://…` |
+| `NODE_OPTIONS=--no-experimental-webstorage` | `packages/nextjs/package.json` (`dev`, `start`, `build`) | On Node 25, the built-in `localStorage` global has no working methods, and wallet libraries crash on it during prerender. Needs Node >= 22.4. |
+
+## 0. Prerequisite: TipJar deployed on Base
+
+The static site reads the TipJar address from `packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts` (key `8453`).
+Deploying to Base is a **real mainnet transaction**. Review the recipient and the deployer account before you run it.
+
+```bash
+# Deployer keystore with a little ETH on Base (yarn generate / yarn account:import, check with yarn account)
+TIP_RECIPIENT=0x<team-multisig-or-wallet> yarn deploy --network base --keystore <keystore-name>
+
+# Confirm the deployment and its wiring
+JAR=$(jq -r 'to_entries[] | select(.value=="TipJar") | .key' packages/foundry/deployments/8453.json)
+cast call $JAR "usdc()(address)"      --rpc-url https://mainnet.base.org   # 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913
+cast call $JAR "recipient()(address)" --rpc-url https://mainnet.base.org   # the intended recipient
+grep -n "8453" packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts               # Base entry exists
+```
+
+Commit the updated `deployedContracts.ts` so the build can be reproduced.
+
+## 1. Build
+
+Run this from the repo root with Node >= 22.4. `yarn ipfs:build` runs `rm -rf .next out`, then lint, then `next build` with `NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true`.
+
+```bash
+NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL="https://<production-domain>" \
+NEXT_PUBLIC_ALCHEMY_API_KEY="<own-alchemy-key>" \
+NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID="<own-walletconnect-project-id>" \
+  yarn ipfs:build
+```
+
+- `<production-domain>` is the URL people will actually open, for example an ENS gateway like `tipjar.eth.limo` or a DNSLink domain.
+- Use your own Alchemy key. The shared default key gets rate-limited (HTTP 429) on Base, and the page reads from Base RPC in the browser.
+
+## 2. Check the build before upload
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+
+# Each route has its own directory with an index.html
+ls out/index.html out/*/index.html
+#   out/index.html  out/debug/index.html  out/blockexplorer/index.html  out/404/index.html ...
+
+# Open Graph uses the production origin, not localhost
+grep -o 'og:url" content="[^"]*"\|og:image" content="[^"]*"' out/index.html out/debug/index.html
+grep -rl "localhost:3000" out --include=*.html && echo "❌ localhost leaked" || echo "✅ no localhost origins"
+
+# The Base TipJar address is baked into the bundle
+JAR=$(jq -r 'to_entries[] | select(.value=="TipJar") | .key' ../foundry/deployments/8453.json)
+grep -rli "${JAR#0x}" out/_next/static/chunks | head -1 || echo "❌ Base TipJar address missing"
+
+# Smoke test the static files locally, then open http://localhost:8080/ and /debug/
+# (wallet should prompt for Base; the tip feed and totals load from Base)
+python3 -m http.server 8080 -d out
+```
+
+## 3. Upload (manual, run only when you intend to publish)
+
+Using BuidlGuidl IPFS (`bgipfs`, already a devDependency):
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+yarn bgipfs upload config init -u https://upload.bgipfs.com   # one-time; asks for your bgipfs API key
+yarn bgipfs upload out                                         # prints "CID: <cid>"
+```
+
+Using your own IPFS node (kubo) and a pinning service instead:
+
+```bash
+CID=$(ipfs add -r -Q --cid-version 1 packages/nextjs/out)
+ipfs pin remote add --service=<pinning-service> --name=tipjar-$(git rev-parse --short HEAD) $CID
+```
+
+Write down the CID together with the git commit it was built from.
+If you get the same CID as the previous release, the uploaded bytes are identical. That means the build didn't change (or the wrong `out/` was uploaded), not that the gateway is caching.
+
+## 4. Check the live site after upload
+
+The export uses root-absolute asset paths (`/_next/...`). Load it from a **subdomain gateway** or from your domain, never from a `/ipfs/<cid>/` path URL. A path URL can return 200 for the HTML while every script 404s.
+
+```bash
+CID=<cid>
+GW="https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link"   # or https://$CID.ipfs.community.bgipfs.com / your production domain
+
+# Root plus the non-home routes all resolve (expect 200 for each, not 404)
+for p in "" debug/ blockexplorer/; do
+  printf "%s /%s\n" "$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' -L "$GW/$p")" "$p"
+done
+
+# JS assets actually load (catches the path-gateway / wrong-root failure)
+ASSET=$(curl -sL "$GW/" | grep -o '/_next/static/[^"]*\.js' | head -1)
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code} $ASSET\n" "$GW$ASSET"          # expect 200
+
+# Open Graph points at the production origin
+curl -sL "$GW/" | grep -o 'og:url" content="[^"]*"\|og:image" content="[^"]*"'
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code} og:image\n" "https://<production-domain>/thumbnail.jpg"
+```
+
+Then open the gateway URL in a browser with a Base wallet and confirm:
+
+1. The network shown is Base, and the "Tips go to" address is the intended recipient.
+2. The totals and tip feed load, with no RPC errors in the console.
+3. A small real tip (such as 0.01 USDC) goes through Approve & tip and appears in the feed. Check it with
+   `cast call $JAR "tipCount()(uint256)" --rpc-url https://mainnet.base.org`.
+
+## 5. ENS (optional)
+
+Update the ENS content hash (`ipfs://<cid>`) **only after the CID above has been reviewed and approved**. Then confirm:
+
+```bash
+cast resolve-name <name>.eth                                        # name resolves
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://<name>.eth.limo/debug/"   # 200 via public ENS gateway
+```
+
+It's also worth checking that the content hash in the ENS manager app shows the same CID you approved.
+
+Don't call the release complete until sections 2 and 4 pass.
+
+## Known limitation
+
+The tip feed reads `Tip` events straight from Base RPC, in batches of 500 blocks, starting at the deployment block. That's fine at launch. But Base produces about 43k blocks per day, so the first load gets slower over time. When that starts to hurt, move the feed to an indexer (see `.agents/skills/ponder`).
